Z||000000||Gary W||10-20-2001||09:14 PM||||Virgin Alert!<BR>Snagged an '86 GT for the cost of towing it to my house. 36K miles, 4sp., all power, sunroof, factory wing. Owner gave up on it because it kept stalling. It's been in a field for 2 years. Paint's shot,but no body damage. Interior VG, considering. Spent the day replacing all ignition components - rats like silicone plug wires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Ran out of daylight, but now I've got no fuel pressure on the rail. Fuel pump or relay tomorrow.<P>Running total: $415 - I'm hooked.||||reg||14
Z||000001||Philphine||10-20-2001||09:39 PM||||welcome, congrats, and yes sounds like a good deal. if it is a fuel pump and you do it yourself you're about $100 away from having it running. don't know what else you might need/want to do but i'd say you're well ahead of the game.<P> i just paid $1200 for my '86gt and it took about $150 more to make it driveable. still need to do some maintenance/personal style stuff but i feel like i found a good deal. you cut my initial buget by about half. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gary W||10-20-2001||09:47 PM||||I plan to do everything but the paint myself. Seems like a good winter project. I hope it fires with the new fuel pump - pushing it around the yard is a pain.||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||10-20-2001||10:07 PM||||I got my fiero for $300 from a guy who had it sitting in his yard for a couple years.  $10.00 (pickup coil) later and some fiddling it runs strong.  70k mile 85GT.  What kind of rust damage do you guys have on these cheap fieros that have been sitting for a long time?  Mine has quite a bit of rust in places.  Below the windshield, around the door hinges, cradle, Sun roof pieces, exaust hangers.  How easy is it to repair this sort of rust damage?  <P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTFiero1||10-20-2001||10:09 PM||||welcome to the forum. that is one great find. i wish that would have happened to me. Fieros are rare here in NJ especially cheap ones<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gary W||10-20-2001||10:14 PM||||Yeah, they need to rename NJ "The Ricer State". Too many Civics that sound like weedwhackers. Here in NW Jersey, I don't see any Fieros around. Plan to change that soon.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||10-20-2001||10:16 PM||||the only really bad rust i've noticed on this car is the battery tray and some of the area beneath it. the car had been pawned and sat for 1 1/2-2 years but as far as i can tell up to the point it was pawned it was cared for pretty well.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gary W||10-20-2001||10:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by beer_stud_21:<BR><B>I got my fiero for $300 from a guy who had it sitting in his yard for a couple years.  $10.00 (pickup coil) later and some fiddling it runs strong.  70k mile 85GT.  What kind of rust damage do you guys have on these cheap fieros that have been sitting for a long time?  Mine has quite a bit of rust in places.  Below the windshield, around the door hinges, cradle, Sun roof pieces, exaust hangers.  How easy is it to repair this sort of rust damage?  <P>-Marlin</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My coil was melted - probably the original problem with the car. So far, rust has been light on mine. Exhaust hangers will rust the day you put them on. Check under your trunk carpet for another problem area. If the parts are not rusted through, clean them off with a wire brush & repaint with a primer for rusted metal and proper paint. Do it now - rust never sleeps.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gary W||10-20-2001||10:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>the only really bad rust i've noticed on this car is the battery tray and some of the area beneath it. the car had been pawned and sat for 1 1/2-2 years but as far as i can tell up to the point it was pawned it was cared for pretty well.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Battery tray? Is that what that brown lump used to be? Guess I need one. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gary W||10-20-2001||10:51 PM||||Added bonus! Just found the 1986 GM Factory Service Manual in the front storage compartment under the spare.||||reg||1
Z||000010||beer_stud_21||10-20-2001||11:30 PM||||Yeah, my battery tray is rusted to hell too.  The factory air cleaner was worse tho.  It was the first thing I tore out of the car when I got it home.  I think its worth it though.  It really opens up that corner of the engine compartment.  I am planning on moving the battery to the front next to open up the other side.  How about the brakes?  Mine are really rusty and don't grab evenly.  Can they be rebuilt or what would be the best/cheapest way to get them in working order?  I've got quite a few questions for people.  Lets keep this thread going.<P>-marlin||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gary W||10-20-2001||11:39 PM||||If you don't know much about brakes, it's best to pay someone who does - too much at stake. If you're going to go for it yourself, rebuilt calipers are fairly cheap. The rotors can be removed and cut. Brake fluid absorbs water, so some people recommend bleeding the entire system every 2 years or so.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Smoooooth GT||10-21-2001||02:23 AM||||Gary, Great find..  Welcome to the Forum!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Steve<P>------------------<BR>NOS Injected 2.8L V/6<BR>UNDER CONSTRUCTION   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooth_sig2_4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Back On Holiday||10-21-2001||12:08 PM||||GTFiero1, Fiero Jon is always finding cheap fieros in mid-east NJ.<P>Battery Tray - I bought a new setup because the entire area around the battery tray and the tray assembly was rotted to hell.  I pulled the fiberglass insulation off and that section is just a shaped piece of thin metal.  not much left there when I got to it, removed a majority of the rusted sections and the Local mechanic who's 1/8 mile down the road, MIG welded a replacement piece of metal that he shaped and he MIG welded the tray and assembly down on it, painted it primer gray and sprayed rubberized undercoating on the wheelwell section.  He did that when I had the entire engine compartment empty, made it a lot easier   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   - It looks great and he did a nice job.  Gave him $100  plus a case of cold Coors lite for 2hrs work in august 90' temp in my hot garage - When you support your neighborhood Garage, they take care of you...wife had "Engine service soon" light on, took it over he hooked it up and came up "EGR #3 Bad"  even gave me advise to fix it...Bet your local GM dealership, or in-town garage wouldn't do that - they rip you off $60 to plug in their machine for 1 mins work.<P><BR>------------------<BR>84se Silver 2m4<BR>86se Silver 2m6<p>[This message has been edited by Back On Holiday (edited 10-21-2001).]||||reg||1
